					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/feathersnake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(669401); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=669401&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973867" alt="feathersnake" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=669401)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/feathersnake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(669401); return false;" >feathersnake</a></span> <span class="odate time_1347295222 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 Sep 2012 16:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1561218">
									

<p>We have tried other types of rocket than liquid fuel and solid fuel- for example, <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Project_Rover">Solid-Core Nuclear Thermal Rockets</a>, which would offer far greater thrust for less fuel. But the word which starts with n and ends with -uclear still makes people run around screaming, despite that a solid-core's exhaust would only be the propellant (probably hydrogen).</p>
<p>And the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orion_drive">Orion Drive</a>- throw nukes behind a pusher plate, they explode and produce thrust- is a very viable design, even if the layman would think you insane for using it. Of course, it couldn't be used to lift off because they are nukes, but it offers very high thrust for low mass ratios.</p>
<p>Ion drives don't have the acceleration to get people to other planets on reasonable timescales, unfortunately.</p>
<p>EDIT: And thirding about the FTL. Obviously, you'd have to be taking LSD of a grade well above any I ever heard of to assume you could say what 100 years of research could achieve, but right now there's no reason to think we'll ever have FTL.</p>

							</div>

								
