															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gavon" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1347106); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1347106&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973867" alt="Gavon" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1347106)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gavon" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1347106); return false;" >Gavon</a></span> <span class="odate time_1347343923 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Sep 2012 06:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1561714">
									

<p>Actually - though I'm not sure how reliable this is - Cracked did mention an experiment where tiny amounts of mass traveled between two points supposedly faster than light.<br />
This is obviously highly suspect information and may simply be a case of faulty equipment, but it's still a reminder that we shouldn't assume everything to work according to a certain theory.</p>
<p>Of course, even if nothing CAN reach the speed of light, most examples of plausible FTL drives in fiction do not involve that, but rather ways of getting around it, like warping space (reducing the actual distance you need to travel), dimensional jumps, teleportation or wormholes - none of which violate the hard rule of nothing ever reaching the speed of light.</p>
<p>Of course, none of these methods may be possible at all, and the only chance we have of reaching the stars are generational ships, which is why I used the phrase &quot;arguably&quot;.<br />
We really have no idea, but if they ARE possible it seems a long leap of logic to assume we are thousands of years away from them with the leaps technology has been making lately.</p>

							</div>

								
					
