															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/feathersnake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(669401); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=669401&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608973867" alt="feathersnake" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=669401)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/feathersnake" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(669401); return false;" >feathersnake</a></span> <span class="odate time_1347354544 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Sep 2012 09:09</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1561858">
									

<p>Trusting Cracked aside&#8230;. :P Those neutrinos? Yeah, that was an experimental error. The fact that they were actually travelling at predicted speeds only provides more evidence for current physics.</p>
<p>These 'plausible' FTL drives tend to have disagreements with rather basic physics such as the conservation of energy, and usually invoke the existence of negative matter, which doesn't really help their cause. Sure, we've found negative energy densities, but negative matter?</p>
<p>We do have an idea, but it's just not complete. I can't say what we'll be able to know or do in the future, but I can say how well they fit with modern physics, which works. I would bet that if we ever develop the means to bypass the trouble with light speed, it will be by a different method than any yet suggested.</p>

							</div>

								
					
